<chapter xml:id="ch-command-line-tool">
  <title>Writing a Command Line tool</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Throughout this text, we have written small chunks of code that was recommended to run inside a Jupyter notebook.
      This chapter will introduce how to write a command line tool.
    </p>

    <p>
      A typical command line tool does one thing
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-arg-parse">
    <title>The <c>ArgParse</c> package</title>

    <p>
      To get start, let's look at an example.
      The following should be saved in a file with a <c>.jl</c> extension.
      Let's save this as <c>example-clt.jl</c>
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="list-example-clt">
      <title>An example Command Line Tool</title>

      <program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
        <code>
        using ArgParse

        function parse_commandline()
          s = ArgParseSettings()
          @add_arg_table s begin
            "--verbose", "-v"
              help = "show additional information"
              action = :store_true
            "--opt1", "-o"
              help = "option one is an integer"
              arg_type = Int
              default = 0
            "--opt2", "-s"
              help = "this is a string option"
              arg_type = String
              default = ""
            "arg1"
              help = "first positional argument"
              required = true
          end
          return parse_args(s)
        end

        function main()
          parsed_args = parse_commandline()
          println("Parsed args:")
          for (arg,val) in parsed_args
            println("  $arg  =&gt;  $val")
          end
        end

        main()
        </code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      and you will need to download and install the <c>ArgParse.jl</c> package.
      To run this, you'll need to use a command line (like Terminal on MacOS or linux or PowerShell on windows.) Also,
      make sure you are in the directory containing the file and enter:
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          julia example-clt.jl
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and you should see the results:
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>required argument arg1 was not provided</cline>
        <cline>usage: example-clt.jl [-v] [-o OPT1] [-s OPT2] arg1</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      This means that we needed to put something on the command line.
      Let's rerun with
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          julia example-clt.jl hello
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      and the result for this is
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>Parsed args:</cline>
        <cline>opt2  =&gt;  </cline>
        <cline>opt1  =&gt;  0</cline>
        <cline>arg1  =&gt;  hello</cline>
        <cline>verbose  =&gt;  false</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      which ran successfully and just output all of the optional arguments.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before getting into the details of the <c>ArgParse</c> package, there is also a built-in option <c>--help</c> or
      <c>-h</c> that will show the details.
      Running
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
          julia example-clt.jl --help
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      results in
    </p>

    <p>
      <cd>
        <cline>usage: example-clt.jl [-v] [-o OPT1] [-s OPT2] [-h] arg1</cline>
        <cline/>
        <cline>positional arguments:</cline>
        <cline>  arg1             first positional argument</cline>
        <cline/>
        <cline>optional arguments:</cline>
        <cline>  -v, --verbose    show additional information</cline>
        <cline>  -o, --opt1 OPT1  option one is an integer (type: Int64, default: 0)</cline>
        <cline>  -s, --opt2 OPT2  this is a string option (default: "")</cline>
        <cline>  -h, --help       show this help message and exit</cline>
      </cd>
    </p>

    <p>
      and this shows all of the options and the help messages.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-arg-parse-table">
    <title>Parsing the Arguments and the Argument Table</title>

    <p>
      There are three steps to effectively create a command line tools.
      In the example in
    </p>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title><c>ArgParseSettings</c></title>

          <p>
            The <c>ArgParseSettings</c> parses the arguments that are entered and creates a number of default settings.
            One such is <c>add_help</c>, which defaults to true is how the <c>--help</c> option creates a short help for
            your command line.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><c>@add_arg_table</c></title>

          <p>
            This creates an argument table.
            This includes the long and short option or argument names, the type of argument, the help string and a
            default value.
            Details on options for this are below.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><c>parse_args</c></title>

          <p>
            This function parses all of the arguments with any restrictions created with the previous argument.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      Notice in <xref ref="list-example-clt"/> these are all called within the <c>parse_commandline</c> function on
      lines 3-22.
      This is fairly standard and a good workflow.
      To change the options available, the table needs to be updated.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each command line option or argument is a block with a long name and short name (you need at least one of these),
      then a set of keywords that set the help and how to parse it.
      For example, the first one in <xref ref="list-example-clt"/> is
    </p>

    <program language="julia">
      <code>
        "--verbose", "-v"
          help = "show additional information"
          action = :store_true
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Line 1 is a pair of strings.
      The first is the long name and needs to start with two dashes: <c>--</c> and the second is a short name with a
      single dash and a single letter.
      If the dashes are missing in the strings, then these are arguments.
      The rest of the block are keyword options.
      Here's a list of them
    </p>

    <p>
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li>
          <title><c>help</c></title>

          <p>
            This is a string that is output if the <c>--help</c> option is shown.
            In general it is a short description of the option or argument.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><c>arg_type</c></title>

          <p>
            This determines how to parse the argument.
            Generally, if this isn't present, the argument is a string, since that is how the arguments are parsed.
            If you want the argument to be interpreted as an integer <c>arg_type = Int</c> is common.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><c>action</c></title>

          <p>
            The <c>:store_arg</c> is the default and that is that the argument is stored.
            This is standard if there is an option of the style <c>--opt1=17</c> for example.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the presences of the flag without a value, generally using <c>:store_true</c> or <c>:store_false</c> is
            used.
            The former means that if the flag is present, store the value as <c>true</c>.
            If the latter is used, then <c>false</c> is stored if the flag is present.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><c>default</c>          </title>

          <p>
            The default value if the flag isn't present.
            This can be what type is appropriate for the flag.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title><c>required</c></title>

          <p>
            Either <c>true</c> if the flag is require or <c>false</c> if not.
            <c>false</c> is the default for this.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-parse-nba-data">
    <title>Parsing NBA data</title>

    <p>
      As an example, we will create a command line tool that will download some HTML files related to NBA scores and
      parse them.
      We will build up the command line tool to do this and at the bottom of this chapter will be a link to the complete
      tool.
    </p>

    <p>
      As an example, consider
      <url href="https://www.basketball-reference.com/leagues/NBA_2026_games-october.html">the Basketball-reference.com site for October 2025</url>.
      There is a table below that lists all the games played in October 2025.
      We will first start with downloading this data and then we will parse it and save it as a CSV file.
      Note: in <xref ref="ch-sports-database"/> will will add the ability to read the data into a database and do some
      analysis.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>